WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:00.086 --> 00:02.126 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference January 27,

00:02.126 --> 00:03.086 align:middle
2021 CHAIR POWELL.

00:04.616 --> 00:04.976 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:04.976 --> 00:07.526 align:middle
At the Federal Reserve,
we are strongly committed

00:07.526 --> 00:09.516 align:middle
to achieving the
monetary policy goals

00:09.516 --> 00:10.656 align:middle
that Congress has given us:

00:11.156 --> 00:13.136 align:middle
maximum employment
and price stability.

00:14.466 --> 00:15.956 align:middle
Since the beginning
of the pandemic,

00:16.356 --> 00:19.876 align:middle
we have taken forceful actions
to provide relief and stability,

00:20.886 --> 00:23.466 align:middle
to ensure that the recovery
will be as strong as possible,

00:24.136 --> 00:28.906 align:middle
and to limit lasting
damage to the economy.

00:29.016 --> 00:30.906 align:middle
Today my colleagues on the FOMC

00:30.906 --> 00:33.136 align:middle
and I kept interest
rates near zero

00:33.136 --> 00:35.736 align:middle
and maintained our
sizable asset purchases.

00:36.916 --> 00:39.276 align:middle
These measures, along
with our strong guidance

00:39.276 --> 00:40.896 align:middle
on interest rates and
our balance sheet,

00:41.616 --> 00:43.906 align:middle
will ensure that monetary
policy will continue

00:43.906 --> 00:46.176 align:middle
to deliver powerful
support to the economy

00:46.256 --> 00:47.896 align:middle
until the recovery is complete.

00:48.356 --> 00:51.926 align:middle
The path of the economy
continues

00:51.926 --> 00:54.786 align:middle
to depend significantly on
the course of the virus.

00:55.356 --> 00:59.056 align:middle
A resurgence in recent
months in COVID-19 cases,

00:59.056 --> 01:02.746 align:middle
hospitalizations, and deaths
is causing great hardship

01:02.746 --> 01:05.386 align:middle
for millions of Americans
and is weighing

01:05.386 --> 01:07.506 align:middle
on economic activity
and job creation.

01:09.156 --> 01:12.416 align:middle
Following a sharp rebound in
economic activity last summer,

01:12.916 --> 01:15.556 align:middle
the pace of the recovery has
moderated in recent months,

01:16.096 --> 01:18.186 align:middle
with the weakness
concentrated in the sectors

01:18.186 --> 01:21.756 align:middle
of the economy most adversely
affected by the resurgence

01:21.756 --> 01:24.036 align:middle
of the virus and by
greater social distancing.

01:25.916 --> 01:28.506 align:middle
Household spending on
services remains low,

01:28.996 --> 01:31.316 align:middle
especially in sectors that
typically require people

01:31.316 --> 01:34.126 align:middle
to gather closely, including
travel and hospitality.

01:35.126 --> 01:38.446 align:middle
And household spending on goods
has moderated following earlier

01:38.446 --> 01:39.256 align:middle
large gains.

01:39.826 --> 01:44.526 align:middle
In contrast, the housing sector
has more than fully recovered

01:44.526 --> 01:46.756 align:middle
from the downturn,
supported in part

01:46.826 --> 01:48.436 align:middle
by low mortgage interest rates.

01:49.436 --> 01:50.286 align:middle
Business investment

01:50.286 --> 01:52.486 align:middle
and manufacturing production
have also picked up.

01:53.656 --> 01:55.796 align:middle
The overall recovery
in economic activity

01:55.796 --> 01:59.816 align:middle
since last spring is due in part
to federal stimulus payments

01:59.976 --> 02:01.926 align:middle
and expanded unemployment
benefits,

02:02.266 --> 02:04.046 align:middle
which have provided
essential support

02:04.046 --> 02:05.746 align:middle
to many families
and individuals.

02:07.916 --> 02:10.556 align:middle
The recently enacted
Coronavirus Response

02:10.556 --> 02:11.286 align:middle
and Relief Act [Consolidated
Appropriations Act,

02:11.826 --> 02:13.166 align:middle
2021] will provide
additional support.

02:15.126 --> 02:18.526 align:middle
Overall economic activity
remains below its level before

02:18.526 --> 02:21.436 align:middle
the pandemic, and the path
ahead remains highly uncertain.

02:23.276 --> 02:25.506 align:middle
As with overall economic
activity,

02:25.616 --> 02:26.626 align:middle
the pace of improvement

02:26.626 --> 02:28.946 align:middle
in the labor market has
slowed in recent months.

02:30.336 --> 02:34.386 align:middle
Employment fell by 140,000 in
December, as continued gains

02:34.386 --> 02:37.696 align:middle
in many industries were
outweighed by significant losses

02:38.106 --> 02:39.686 align:middle
in industries where
the resurgence

02:39.686 --> 02:41.816 align:middle
of the virus has weighed
further on activity.

02:43.006 --> 02:44.266 align:middle
In particular, the leisure

02:44.266 --> 02:47.266 align:middle
and hospitality sector lost
nearly half a million jobs,

02:47.336 --> 02:49.126 align:middle
largely from restaurants
and bars.

02:49.636 --> 02:53.676 align:middle
The unemployment rate remained
elevated at 6.7 percent

02:53.676 --> 02:55.596 align:middle
in December, and participation

02:55.596 --> 02:58.926 align:middle
in the labor market is notably
below pre-pandemic levels.

03:00.916 --> 03:03.216 align:middle
Although there has been much
progress in the labor market

03:03.216 --> 03:06.466 align:middle
since the spring, millions of
Americans remain out of work.

03:07.076 --> 03:11.336 align:middle
The economic downturn has not
fallen equally on all Americans,

03:11.816 --> 03:14.406 align:middle
and those least able to shoulder
the burden have been the

03:14.406 --> 03:15.016 align:middle
hardest hit.

03:15.916 --> 03:17.426 align:middle
In particular, the high level

03:17.426 --> 03:19.826 align:middle
of joblessness has
been especially severe

03:19.826 --> 03:22.136 align:middle
for lower-wage workers
in the service sector

03:22.826 --> 03:24.916 align:middle
and for African Americans
and Hispanics.

03:25.496 --> 03:30.336 align:middle
The economic dislocation
has upended many lives

03:30.876 --> 03:33.196 align:middle
and created great
uncertainty about the future.

03:33.736 --> 03:37.336 align:middle
The pandemic has also
left a significant imprint

03:37.336 --> 03:37.986 align:middle
on inflation.

03:39.196 --> 03:42.356 align:middle
Following large declines in the
spring, consumer prices picked

03:42.356 --> 03:44.966 align:middle
up over the summer but have
leveled out more recently.

03:45.846 --> 03:48.296 align:middle
For those sectors that have
been most adversely affected

03:48.296 --> 03:51.396 align:middle
by the pandemic, prices
remain particularly soft.

03:52.646 --> 03:54.426 align:middle
Overall, on a 12-month basis,

03:54.426 --> 03:57.566 align:middle
inflation remains below our 2
percent longer-run objective.

03:58.986 --> 04:00.946 align:middle
While we should not
underestimate the challenges we

04:00.946 --> 04:03.406 align:middle
currently face, several
developments point

04:03.406 --> 04:06.056 align:middle
to an improved outlook
for later this year.

04:07.256 --> 04:09.996 align:middle
Sufficiently widespread
vaccinations would enable us

04:09.996 --> 04:12.556 align:middle
to put the pandemic
behind us and return

04:12.556 --> 04:14.566 align:middle
to more normal economic
activities.

04:15.556 --> 04:17.356 align:middle
In the meantime,
continued observance

04:17.356 --> 04:18.816 align:middle
of social-distancing measures

04:18.816 --> 04:21.026 align:middle
and wearing masks
will help us reach

04:21.026 --> 04:22.946 align:middle
that goal as soon as possible.

04:24.796 --> 04:27.076 align:middle
Support from fiscal
policy will help households

04:27.076 --> 04:29.596 align:middle
and businesses weather
the downturn as well

04:29.596 --> 04:31.436 align:middle
as limit lasting
damage to the economy

04:31.436 --> 04:33.206 align:middle
that could otherwise
impede the recovery.

04:34.396 --> 04:36.776 align:middle
In addition, as we have
seen since last summer,

04:37.266 --> 04:39.656 align:middle
the economy has proved more
resilient than expected,

04:39.656 --> 04:41.466 align:middle
in part reflecting
the adaptability

04:41.466 --> 04:42.726 align:middle
of households and businesses.

04:43.666 --> 04:46.836 align:middle
Finally, monetary policy
is playing a key role

04:47.086 --> 04:49.696 align:middle
in supporting the recovery
and will continue to do so.

04:52.196 --> 04:54.686 align:middle
The Fed's response to this
crisis has been guided

04:54.686 --> 04:57.406 align:middle
by our mandate to
promote maximum employment

04:57.406 --> 04:59.336 align:middle
and stable prices for
the American people,

04:59.876 --> 05:02.416 align:middle
along with our responsibilities
to promote the stability

05:02.416 --> 05:03.326 align:middle
of the financial system.

05:04.356 --> 05:07.366 align:middle
Today we unanimously
reaffirmed our Statement

05:07.366 --> 05:10.176 align:middle
on Longer-Run Goals and
Monetary Policy Strategy,

05:10.296 --> 05:12.026 align:middle
as we typically do each January.

05:13.226 --> 05:16.386 align:middle
As we say in that statement,
we view maximum employment as a

05:16.386 --> 05:18.226 align:middle
"broad-based and
inclusive goal."

05:19.596 --> 05:21.566 align:middle
Our ability to achieve
maximum employment

05:21.566 --> 05:23.666 align:middle
in the years ahead
depends importantly

05:24.026 --> 05:26.936 align:middle
on having longer-term inflation
expectations well anchored

05:26.936 --> 05:27.686 align:middle
at 2 percent.

05:28.176 --> 05:31.896 align:middle
As the Committee reiterated
in today's policy statement,

05:32.026 --> 05:34.606 align:middle
with inflation running
persistently below 2 percent,

05:35.196 --> 05:38.526 align:middle
we will aim to achieve inflation
moderately above 2 percent

05:38.526 --> 05:42.746 align:middle
for some time so that inflation
averages 2 percent over time

05:42.746 --> 05:45.666 align:middle
and longer-term inflation
expectations remain well

05:45.666 --> 05:47.036 align:middle
anchored at 2 percent.

05:47.466 --> 05:50.906 align:middle
We expect to maintain
an accommodative stance

05:50.906 --> 05:53.786 align:middle
of monetary policy
until these employment

05:53.786 --> 05:55.566 align:middle
and inflation outcomes
are achieved.

05:56.806 --> 05:58.216 align:middle
With regard to interest rates,

05:58.476 --> 06:00.936 align:middle
we continue to expect
it will be appropriate

06:00.936 --> 06:04.186 align:middle
to maintain the current 0
to ¼ percent target range

06:04.186 --> 06:05.206 align:middle
for the federal funds rate

06:05.976 --> 06:09.036 align:middle
until labor market conditions
have reached levels consistent

06:09.036 --> 06:11.146 align:middle
with the Committee's
assessment of maximum employment

06:11.816 --> 06:14.676 align:middle
and inflation has risen to
2 percent and is on track

06:14.676 --> 06:19.146 align:middle
to moderately exceed 2
percent for some time.

06:19.346 --> 06:22.216 align:middle
In addition, we will continue
to increase our holdings

06:22.216 --> 06:25.706 align:middle
of Treasury securities by at
least $80 billion per month

06:26.286 --> 06:28.136 align:middle
and of agency mortgage-backed
securities

06:28.136 --> 06:30.086 align:middle
by at least $40 billion
per month

06:30.756 --> 06:33.936 align:middle
until substantial further
progress has been made toward

06:33.936 --> 06:36.616 align:middle
our maximum-employment
and price-stability goals.

06:37.376 --> 06:39.086 align:middle
The increase in our
balance sheet

06:39.086 --> 06:42.016 align:middle
since last March has materially
eased financial conditions

06:42.526 --> 06:45.036 align:middle
and is providing substantial
support to the economy.

06:46.486 --> 06:48.936 align:middle
The economy is a long
way from our employment

06:48.936 --> 06:51.766 align:middle
and inflation goals, and it
is likely to take some time

06:51.976 --> 06:54.256 align:middle
for substantial further
progress to be achieved.

06:54.706 --> 06:58.466 align:middle
Our forward guidance for
the federal funds rate,

06:58.526 --> 07:00.126 align:middle
along with our balance
sheet guidance,

07:00.616 --> 07:01.776 align:middle
will ensure that the stance

07:01.776 --> 07:04.466 align:middle
of monetary policy remains
highly accommodative

07:04.496 --> 07:05.966 align:middle
as the recovery progresses.

07:06.896 --> 07:09.606 align:middle
Our guidance is outcome
based and ties the path

07:09.606 --> 07:11.456 align:middle
of the federal funds rate
and the balance sheet

07:11.876 --> 07:14.986 align:middle
to progress toward reaching our
employment and inflation goals.

07:15.676 --> 07:17.886 align:middle
Thus, if progress toward
our goals were to slow,

07:18.626 --> 07:20.286 align:middle
the guidance would
convey our intention

07:20.286 --> 07:23.916 align:middle
to increase policy accommodation
through a lower expected path

07:23.916 --> 07:24.996 align:middle
of the federal funds rate

07:25.296 --> 07:27.616 align:middle
and a higher expected
path of the balance sheet.

07:28.846 --> 07:30.266 align:middle
Overall, our interest rate

07:30.266 --> 07:32.996 align:middle
and balance sheet tools are
providing powerful support

07:32.996 --> 07:35.246 align:middle
to the economy and
will continue to do so.

07:36.876 --> 07:39.866 align:middle
We've also taken actions to
more directly support the flow

07:39.866 --> 07:40.956 align:middle
of credit in the economy,

07:41.376 --> 07:43.336 align:middle
deploying our emergency
lending powers

07:43.336 --> 07:46.386 align:middle
to an unprecedented extent,
enabled in large part

07:46.466 --> 07:49.746 align:middle
by financial backing and support
from Congress and the Treasury.

07:50.846 --> 07:53.356 align:middle
Although the CARES Act
facilities are no-are no longer

07:53.356 --> 07:56.506 align:middle
open to new activity, our other
facilities remain in place.

07:57.756 --> 08:01.106 align:middle
To conclude, we understand that
our actions affect communities,

08:01.156 --> 08:03.256 align:middle
families, and businesses
across the country.

08:04.106 --> 08:06.736 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

08:07.266 --> 08:09.866 align:middle
We are committed to using
our full range of tools

08:09.866 --> 08:12.966 align:middle
to support the economy and to
help assure that the recovery

08:12.966 --> 08:16.146 align:middle
from this difficult period
will be as robust as possible.

08:17.366 --> 08:17.776 align:middle
Thank you.

08:17.776 --> 08:18.896 align:middle
I look forward to
your questions.

08:19.126 --> 08:19.746 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

08:20.116 --> 08:20.496 align:middle
Thank you.

08:21.596 --> 08:21.976 align:middle
Jeanna Smialek.

08:22.006 --> 08:22.606 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

08:22.806 --> 08:25.766 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

08:25.766 --> 08:26.906 align:middle
Jeanna Smialek from
the New York Times.

08:26.906 --> 08:29.726 align:middle
Thank you for taking
our questions.

08:29.726 --> 08:32.876 align:middle
I was hoping that you would
first react to the wild ride

08:32.876 --> 08:36.336 align:middle
that game stock-GameStop's
stock has had this week.

08:36.406 --> 08:37.766 align:middle
And then, secondarily, you

08:37.766 --> 08:41.696 align:middle
and your colleagues have
repeatedly made it clear

08:42.006 --> 08:44.426 align:middle
that you really plan to
use macroprudential tools

08:44.426 --> 08:45.876 align:middle
as the first line of
defense when it comes

08:45.876 --> 08:47.346 align:middle
to financial stability risks.

08:47.346 --> 08:51.076 align:middle
But your macroprudential tools
primarily apply to the banks.

08:51.156 --> 08:54.996 align:middle
I'm wondering what your plan
is, if you see some sort

08:54.996 --> 08:57.276 align:middle
of large financial
stability risk emanating

08:57.276 --> 08:59.566 align:middle
from the nonbank financial
sector in the coming months,

08:59.566 --> 09:01.076 align:middle
especially as it relates

09:01.076 --> 09:02.766 align:middle
to search-for-yield
kind of activities.

09:02.766 --> 09:06.466 align:middle
You know, what, what do you
see as the solution there?

09:06.466 --> 09:08.246 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

09:08.246 --> 09:12.806 align:middle
So, on, on your first question,
I don't want to comment

09:12.806 --> 09:15.116 align:middle
on a particular company or,

09:15.256 --> 09:18.806 align:middle
or day's market activity
or things like that.

09:18.806 --> 09:20.986 align:middle
It's just not really something

09:20.986 --> 09:23.836 align:middle
that I would typically
comment on.

09:24.276 --> 09:29.226 align:middle
In terms of macroprudential
policy tools, so, as you know,

09:29.226 --> 09:33.416 align:middle
no doubt, we rely on
sort of always-on,

09:33.416 --> 09:35.936 align:middle
through-the-cycle
macroprudential policy tools,

09:35.936 --> 09:39.366 align:middle
particularly the stress tests
and also the elevated levels

09:39.366 --> 09:42.436 align:middle
of liquidity and
capital and, and,

09:42.436 --> 09:46.826 align:middle
and also resolution planning
that we-that we impose

09:46.826 --> 09:48.416 align:middle
on the largest financial
institutions.

09:48.796 --> 09:52.456 align:middle
We don't use time-varying
tests and tools

09:52.456 --> 09:53.426 align:middle
as some other countries do.

09:53.426 --> 09:55.676 align:middle
And we think it's a good
approach because-for us

09:55.676 --> 09:57.606 align:middle
to use ones that are
always-on-because we don't

09:57.606 --> 10:02.336 align:middle
really think we'd be
successful in every case

10:02.336 --> 10:05.316 align:middle
in picking the exact right
time to intervene in markets.

10:05.316 --> 10:06.476 align:middle
So that's for banks.

10:06.856 --> 10:09.266 align:middle
You really asked about
nonbanks, the nonbank sector.

10:09.786 --> 10:14.456 align:middle
And so we monitor financial
conditions very broadly.

10:15.016 --> 10:17.676 align:middle
And while we don't
have jurisdiction over,

10:18.256 --> 10:21.756 align:middle
over many areas in the nonbank
sector, other agencies do.

10:21.756 --> 10:24.576 align:middle
And so we do coordinate
through the Financial Stability

10:24.576 --> 10:26.546 align:middle
Oversight Council and
with other agencies

10:26.546 --> 10:31.076 align:middle
who have responsibility for,
for nonbank supervision.

10:31.076 --> 10:34.386 align:middle
And, in fact, as you
know, in the last crisis,

10:34.566 --> 10:37.466 align:middle
the banking system held
up fairly well so far.

10:37.816 --> 10:40.216 align:middle
And, and the dislocations
that we saw

10:40.216 --> 10:44.096 align:middle
from the outsized economic
and financial shock

10:44.096 --> 10:46.686 align:middle
of the pandemic really
appeared in the nonbank sector.

10:46.686 --> 10:49.156 align:middle
So that's-right now
we are engaged in,

10:49.156 --> 10:52.326 align:middle
in carefully examining,
understanding, and thinking

10:52.326 --> 10:55.856 align:middle
about what, what in the nonbank
sector will need to be addressed

10:55.856 --> 10:57.766 align:middle
in the next year or so.

10:58.226 --> 11:00.976 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

11:00.976 --> 11:01.476 align:middle
Thank you.

11:01.476 --> 11:02.956 align:middle
James Politi, FT.

11:03.086 --> 11:08.606 align:middle
JAMES POLITI.

11:09.036 --> 11:12.106 align:middle
Thank you so much,
Michelle and Chair Powell.

11:12.136 --> 11:14.596 align:middle
I had a question
on fiscal policy.

11:15.336 --> 11:17.336 align:middle
Last year you consistently said

11:17.336 --> 11:21.426 align:middle
that the economy needed more
fiscal support and, I believe,

11:21.426 --> 11:25.056 align:middle
were pleased when the $900
billion package was approved

11:25.056 --> 11:25.886 align:middle
in December.

11:25.886 --> 11:29.196 align:middle
We have-now have a new, new
President, a new Congress.

11:29.196 --> 11:32.246 align:middle
We have a weakening
short-term outlook.

11:32.536 --> 11:36.376 align:middle
Do you believe the economy
still needs additional support

11:36.596 --> 11:37.916 align:middle
on the fiscal side?

11:38.186 --> 11:39.746 align:middle
And in what areas?

11:39.746 --> 11:40.026 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

11:40.126 --> 11:41.486 align:middle
Thank you.

11:41.486 --> 11:45.446 align:middle
So I guess I'd start by saying
that the, the fiscal response

11:45.446 --> 11:48.736 align:middle
that we've seen to this
downturn has been strong,

11:48.736 --> 11:51.376 align:middle
and I think we can say now
that it's been sustained

11:51.606 --> 11:53.276 align:middle
after the passage of the-of the,

11:53.996 --> 11:56.726 align:middle
the most recent act
in, in late December.

11:57.276 --> 11:59.506 align:middle
And that's really a key reason
why the recovery has been

11:59.506 --> 12:00.406 align:middle
as strong as it's been.

12:00.406 --> 12:02.856 align:middle
Fiscal policy has been
absolutely essential.

12:03.186 --> 12:05.246 align:middle
When we look back on, on
the history of this period,

12:05.246 --> 12:08.956 align:middle
we'll see a strong and sustained
fiscal policy response.

12:09.516 --> 12:11.976 align:middle
I would-I would add that
we're, as I mentioned,

12:11.976 --> 12:13.826 align:middle
a long way from a full recovery.

12:14.216 --> 12:17.276 align:middle
Something like 9 million
people remain unemployed

12:17.276 --> 12:18.796 align:middle
as a consequence
of the pandemic.

12:19.166 --> 12:22.166 align:middle
That's as many people as
lost their jobs at the peak

12:22.666 --> 12:25.036 align:middle
of the Global Financial Crisis
and the Great Recession.

12:25.466 --> 12:27.116 align:middle
Many small businesses
are under pressure,

12:27.426 --> 12:29.296 align:middle
and there are other
needs to be addressed.

12:29.826 --> 12:31.646 align:middle
And the path ahead is
still pretty uncertain.

12:32.116 --> 12:35.566 align:middle
So, all of that said, the
judgment on how much to spend

12:35.566 --> 12:38.116 align:middle
and in what way is
really one for Congress

12:38.116 --> 12:39.946 align:middle
and the Administration
and not for the Fed.

12:40.596 --> 12:42.856 align:middle
And these discussions
are going on right now.

12:42.856 --> 12:47.006 align:middle
So there's a discussion, as
you know, right now around,

12:47.006 --> 12:49.636 align:middle
around those precise questions,
and, and that's appropriate,

12:49.726 --> 12:52.926 align:middle
but not for us to play a role in
talking about specific policies.

12:52.926 --> 12:53.536 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

12:56.566 --> 12:57.296 align:middle
Thank you.

12:57.296 --> 12:58.186 align:middle
Steve Liesman.

12:59.906 --> 13:00.466 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

13:00.466 --> 13:01.476 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

13:01.476 --> 13:03.776 align:middle
I wonder if I could follow
up on Jeanna's question here.

13:04.136 --> 13:09.256 align:middle
I understand that you do
address issues of, of valuations

13:09.256 --> 13:11.526 align:middle
through macroprudential
policies in the first instance,

13:11.526 --> 13:13.596 align:middle
but there's a range of assets.

13:13.596 --> 13:15.436 align:middle
And I know you do watch
a range of assets.

13:15.436 --> 13:18.566 align:middle
But from Bitcoin to corporate
bonds to the stock market

13:18.566 --> 13:23.366 align:middle
in general to some of these
more specific meteoric rises

13:23.366 --> 13:27.336 align:middle
in stocks like GameStop, how
do you address the concern

13:27.606 --> 13:30.576 align:middle
that super easy monetary
policy-asset purchases

13:30.576 --> 13:34.576 align:middle
and zero interest rates-are
potentially fueling a bubble

13:34.576 --> 13:37.336 align:middle
that could cause economic
fallout should it burst?

13:37.336 --> 13:37.616 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

13:37.616 --> 13:40.976 align:middle
Let me-let me provide
a little context.

13:41.656 --> 13:45.596 align:middle
The shock that the-from the
pandemic was unprecedented both

13:45.596 --> 13:49.146 align:middle
in its nature and in its
size and in the amount

13:49.146 --> 13:50.516 align:middle
of unemployment that it created

13:50.516 --> 13:52.266 align:middle
and in the shock to
economic activity.

13:52.266 --> 13:55.606 align:middle
There's nothing close to it in
our modern economic history.

13:56.846 --> 13:59.936 align:middle
So our response was
really to that.

14:00.096 --> 14:02.696 align:middle
And we-we've done
what we could, first,

14:02.696 --> 14:06.506 align:middle
to restore market function and
to provide a bit of relief,

14:06.606 --> 14:08.206 align:middle
then to support the recovery.

14:08.206 --> 14:10.466 align:middle
And, hopefully, we'll be
able to do the third thing,

14:10.466 --> 14:13.956 align:middle
which is to avoid longer-run
damage to the-to the economy.

14:14.026 --> 14:17.586 align:middle
Our role assigned by
Congress is maximum employment

14:17.586 --> 14:20.616 align:middle
and stable prices and also
look after financial stability.

14:20.656 --> 14:23.976 align:middle
So, in a world where,
almost a year later, we're,

14:24.096 --> 14:27.306 align:middle
we're still 9 million jobs,
at least-that's one way

14:27.306 --> 14:29.066 align:middle
of counting it; it can
actually be counted much higher

14:29.066 --> 14:32.446 align:middle
than that-short of maximum
employment and people are

14:32.446 --> 14:33.916 align:middle
out of the labor force who were

14:33.916 --> 14:36.196 align:middle
in the labor force-the real
unemployment rate is close

14:36.196 --> 14:38.236 align:middle
to 10 percent if you include
people that've left the labor

14:38.236 --> 14:40.586 align:middle
force-it's very much appropriate

14:41.086 --> 14:43.556 align:middle
that monetary policy
be highly accommodative

14:43.556 --> 14:46.526 align:middle
to support maximum
employment and price stability,

14:46.526 --> 14:48.396 align:middle
which is getting
inflation back to 2 percent

14:48.396 --> 14:49.916 align:middle
and averaging 2 percent
over time.

14:50.576 --> 14:55.326 align:middle
So, on, on matters of financial
stability, we have a framework.

14:55.326 --> 14:57.136 align:middle
We don't look at one
thing or two things.

14:57.136 --> 14:59.016 align:middle
We look at-and we made
that framework public

14:59.016 --> 15:01.356 align:middle
after the financial crisis so
that it could be criticized

15:01.356 --> 15:02.946 align:middle
and understood and we
could be held accountable.

15:03.566 --> 15:04.776 align:middle
And, you know, the
things that we look

15:04.776 --> 15:06.656 align:middle
at are-we do look
at asset prices.

15:06.656 --> 15:08.776 align:middle
We also look at leverage
in the banking system.

15:09.176 --> 15:12.456 align:middle
We look at leverage in the
non-the nonbanking system,

15:12.456 --> 15:15.586 align:middle
which is to say,
corporates and households.

15:16.146 --> 15:18.916 align:middle
And we look at also
funding risk.

15:18.916 --> 15:23.246 align:middle
And if you look at it across
that range of, of, of readings,

15:23.566 --> 15:25.856 align:middle
they're each different, but
we monitor them carefully.

15:25.856 --> 15:29.376 align:middle
And I would say that financial
stability vulnerabilities

15:29.416 --> 15:30.776 align:middle
overall are moderate.

15:31.286 --> 15:34.156 align:middle
Our overall goal is to assure

15:34.156 --> 15:38.106 align:middle
that the financial system
itself is resilient to,

15:38.616 --> 15:42.336 align:middle
to shocks of all kinds, that
it's strong and resilient.

15:42.336 --> 15:43.666 align:middle
And that includes
not just the banks,

15:43.706 --> 15:47.606 align:middle
but money market funds and,
and all different kinds

15:47.656 --> 15:52.496 align:middle
of nonbank financial
structures as well.

15:53.456 --> 16:00.016 align:middle
So when we get to the
nonfinancial sector,

16:00.746 --> 16:03.616 align:middle
we don't-we don't have
jurisdiction over that.

16:03.616 --> 16:09.866 align:middle
But-so I would just say that
our-that there are many things

16:09.866 --> 16:12.296 align:middle
that go in, as you know,
to, to setting asset prices.

16:12.406 --> 16:16.426 align:middle
So if you look at what's really
been driving asset prices,

16:16.426 --> 16:17.896 align:middle
really, in the last
couple of months,

16:18.396 --> 16:19.776 align:middle
it isn't monetary policy.

16:19.776 --> 16:21.886 align:middle
It's been expectations
about vaccines.

16:22.126 --> 16:25.046 align:middle
And it's also financial-sorry,
fiscal policy.

16:25.096 --> 16:28.676 align:middle
Those are-those are the news
items that have been driving,

16:28.706 --> 16:30.956 align:middle
driving asset purchases-sorry,

16:30.956 --> 16:32.876 align:middle
asset values in,
in recent months.

16:33.206 --> 16:36.326 align:middle
So I, I know that monetary
policy does play a role there,

16:36.926 --> 16:38.536 align:middle
but that's how we look at it.

16:38.536 --> 16:44.636 align:middle
And I think, you know, I
think that the connection

16:44.636 --> 16:46.276 align:middle
between low interest rates and,

16:46.276 --> 16:48.706 align:middle
and asset values is probably
something that's not as tight

16:48.706 --> 16:50.796 align:middle
as people think,
because a lot of-a lot

16:50.796 --> 16:53.546 align:middle
of different factors are driving
asset prices at any given time.

16:54.476 --> 16:55.216 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

16:55.216 --> 16:57.196 align:middle
Can I follow up,
Michelle, if you don't mind?

16:57.196 --> 17:00.826 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, do you rule out
or see as one of your tools

17:00.826 --> 17:04.536 align:middle
in the toolkit the idea of
adjusting monetary policy

17:04.536 --> 17:05.766 align:middle
to address asset values?

17:05.766 --> 17:05.996 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

17:06.196 --> 17:09.266 align:middle
So, you know, that's-as
you know,

17:09.336 --> 17:11.926 align:middle
that's one of the very
difficult questions

17:11.926 --> 17:13.236 align:middle
in all of monetary policy.

17:13.236 --> 17:16.336 align:middle
And we don't rule it out as
a theoretical matter, but we,

17:16.336 --> 17:18.716 align:middle
we clearly look to
macroprudential tools,

17:18.716 --> 17:20.856 align:middle
regulatory tools,
supervisory tools,

17:21.626 --> 17:25.036 align:middle
other kinds of tools rather
than monetary policy in,

17:25.886 --> 17:28.816 align:middle
in addressing financial
stability issues.

17:29.276 --> 17:30.506 align:middle
It's not-you know, the,

17:30.506 --> 17:34.446 align:middle
the monetary policy we know
strengthens economic activity

17:34.446 --> 17:37.106 align:middle
and job creation through fairly
well-understood channels.

17:37.346 --> 17:41.106 align:middle
And a strong economy is
actually a great supporter

17:41.106 --> 17:42.176 align:middle
of financial stability.

17:42.176 --> 17:46.176 align:middle
That will mean strong, you know,
well-capitalized institutions,

17:46.296 --> 17:49.416 align:middle
and, and households
will be-will be working.

17:49.416 --> 17:51.706 align:middle
And, and so we know that.

17:51.706 --> 17:54.806 align:middle
We don't actually understand
the tradeoff between-the sense

17:54.806 --> 17:57.326 align:middle
of it is, would you-if
you raise interest rates

17:57.326 --> 18:00.326 align:middle
and thereby tighten
financial conditions

18:00.326 --> 18:03.366 align:middle
and reduce economic
activity now in order

18:03.366 --> 18:05.856 align:middle
to address asset bubbles
and things like that,

18:06.376 --> 18:08.246 align:middle
what-will that even help?

18:08.936 --> 18:11.636 align:middle
Will it-will it actually cause
more damage, or will it help?

18:11.636 --> 18:13.086 align:middle
So I think that's unresolved.

18:13.086 --> 18:17.216 align:middle
And I, I think it's, it's
something we, we look at as not,

18:17.216 --> 18:20.106 align:middle
not theoretically ruled
out but not something we,

18:20.106 --> 18:23.676 align:middle
we-we've ever done and not
something we would plan to do.

18:23.676 --> 18:26.106 align:middle
We would rely on macroprudential
and other tools to deal

18:26.106 --> 18:27.326 align:middle
with financial stability issues.

18:27.376 --> 18:27.756 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

18:28.446 --> 18:28.826 align:middle
Thank you.

18:28.826 --> 18:30.346 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

18:30.716 --> 18:31.156 align:middle
Thank you.

18:31.156 --> 18:31.836 align:middle
Michael Derby.

18:34.116 --> 18:35.156 align:middle
MICHAEL DERBY.

18:35.446 --> 18:36.246 align:middle
Yes. Thank you very much.

18:36.246 --> 18:38.536 align:middle
I wanted to ask you, if
there is a near-term rise

18:38.536 --> 18:40.216 align:middle
in inflation related
to the recovery,

18:40.216 --> 18:41.266 align:middle
how will you determine whether

18:41.266 --> 18:43.336 align:middle
or not it's something that's
temporary or more enduring?

18:43.336 --> 18:46.426 align:middle
And how much inflation
is the Fed willing

18:46.426 --> 18:50.536 align:middle
to tolerate before it acts
to restrain price pressures?

18:50.536 --> 18:52.306 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

18:52.506 --> 18:58.326 align:middle
So, on inflation, a couple
of things-there are a couple

18:58.326 --> 19:01.046 align:middle
of things that are
worth mentioning.

19:01.046 --> 19:04.306 align:middle
One is just that we know
that we measure inflation

19:04.306 --> 19:05.906 align:middle
on a trailing 12-month basis.

19:05.906 --> 19:11.546 align:middle
And as we-as we lap the very
low inflation readings of March

19:11.546 --> 19:12.776 align:middle
and April of last year,

19:13.226 --> 19:16.236 align:middle
we'll see measured 12-month
inflation move up a few tenths.

19:16.786 --> 19:18.536 align:middle
This is just-they're
called base effects, and,

19:18.536 --> 19:22.216 align:middle
and that's a-that's a transient
thing that we think will pass.

19:22.656 --> 19:24.466 align:middle
There's also the
possibility-indeed,

19:24.466 --> 19:28.436 align:middle
it's in some forecasts-that
as the economy fully reopens,

19:28.546 --> 19:29.976 align:middle
there'll be a burst of spending,

19:29.976 --> 19:33.626 align:middle
and-because people
will be enthusiastic

19:33.626 --> 19:35.546 align:middle
that the pandemic is
over, potentially,

19:35.546 --> 19:38.806 align:middle
and that that could also
create some upward pressure

19:38.806 --> 19:39.406 align:middle
on inflation.

19:40.026 --> 19:45.186 align:middle
Now, again, we would see that as
something likely to be transient

19:45.286 --> 19:46.336 align:middle
and not to be very large.

19:46.336 --> 19:48.826 align:middle
In both cases, we don't
see those as either lasting

19:48.826 --> 19:49.876 align:middle
or particularly large.

19:50.206 --> 19:52.606 align:middle
So the way we would react is,
we're going to be patient.

19:52.606 --> 19:57.496 align:middle
Expect us to wait and see
and not react if we see small

19:57.496 --> 20:00.026 align:middle
and what we would review-we
would view as very likely

20:00.026 --> 20:03.246 align:middle
to be transient effects
on inflation.

20:03.746 --> 20:06.756 align:middle
I think if you-you know,
it helps to look back at,

20:06.756 --> 20:10.256 align:middle
at the inflation dynamics that
the United States has had now

20:10.256 --> 20:14.576 align:middle
for some decades and notice
that there has been, you know,

20:14.576 --> 20:17.926 align:middle
significant disinflationary
pressure for some time,

20:17.926 --> 20:19.136 align:middle
for a couple of decades.

20:19.236 --> 20:21.326 align:middle
Inflation has averaged
less than 2 percent

20:21.356 --> 20:22.666 align:middle
for a quarter of a century.

20:23.126 --> 20:25.546 align:middle
And the inflation dynamics,
with the flat Phillips curve

20:25.546 --> 20:28.556 align:middle
and low persistence of
inflation, is very much intact.

20:28.556 --> 20:30.846 align:middle
Those things, they
change over time.

20:30.846 --> 20:33.386 align:middle
We understand that inflation
dynamics evolve constantly

20:33.386 --> 20:35.906 align:middle
over time, but they
don't change rapidly.

20:35.906 --> 20:37.416 align:middle
So we think it's very unlikely

20:37.416 --> 20:39.536 align:middle
that anything we see
now would result in,

20:39.576 --> 20:42.926 align:middle
you know, troubling inflation.

20:42.926 --> 20:47.556 align:middle
Of course, if we did get
sustained inflation at a level

20:47.556 --> 20:49.776 align:middle
that was uncomfortable,
we have tools for that.

20:50.026 --> 20:52.976 align:middle
It's far harder to deal
with, with too low inflation.

20:52.976 --> 20:56.946 align:middle
We know what to do with higher
inflation, which is, you know,

20:57.156 --> 20:59.286 align:middle
should the need arise, we,
we would have those tools.

20:59.326 --> 21:00.806 align:middle
And we don't expect
to see that at all.

21:01.176 --> 21:03.286 align:middle
In terms of how much, you
know, what we've said is we'd

21:03.286 --> 21:06.266 align:middle
like to see-because inflation
has been running persistently

21:06.266 --> 21:10.706 align:middle
below 2 percent, we'd like
to see it run moderately

21:10.706 --> 21:12.426 align:middle
above 2 percent for some time.

21:12.656 --> 21:14.226 align:middle
We have not adopted a formula.

21:14.606 --> 21:16.166 align:middle
We're not going to
adopt a formula.

21:16.246 --> 21:20.216 align:middle
We're going to-we, we use
policy rules and formulas

21:20.216 --> 21:22.496 align:middle
in everything we do,
consult them constantly,

21:22.496 --> 21:23.926 align:middle
but we don't set policy by them.

21:23.956 --> 21:24.566 align:middle
We don't do that.

21:24.966 --> 21:29.456 align:middle
And so we're going to preserve
an element of judgment.

21:30.056 --> 21:34.636 align:middle
And, again, we'll seek
inflation moderately

21:34.636 --> 21:36.556 align:middle
above 2 percent for some time.

21:36.636 --> 21:38.346 align:middle
And we'll, we'll
show what that means

21:38.346 --> 21:39.966 align:middle
when we get inflation
above 2 percent.

21:39.966 --> 21:41.976 align:middle
The, the way to achieve
credibility

21:41.976 --> 21:43.306 align:middle
on that is to actually do it.

21:43.306 --> 21:45.146 align:middle
And so that's what
we're planning on doing.

21:45.146 --> 21:45.736 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

21:47.006 --> 21:47.716 align:middle
Thank you.

21:47.716 --> 21:48.176 align:middle
Rachel Siegel.

21:48.176 --> 21:49.016 align:middle
RACHEL SIEGEL.

21:53.296 --> 21:55.616 align:middle
Thank you, Michelle, and
thank you, Chair Powell,

21:55.616 --> 21:57.196 align:middle
for taking our questions.

21:57.196 --> 22:00.226 align:middle
I have a two-pronged
question about vaccines.

22:00.226 --> 22:03.286 align:middle
So you specifically mentioned
progress in the rollout

22:03.286 --> 22:06.496 align:middle
of vaccines, but there is
still plenty that we don't know

22:06.496 --> 22:09.156 align:middle
about supply over the
coming weeks and months.

22:09.156 --> 22:12.676 align:middle
So I'm curious how you and
your colleagues are factoring

22:12.676 --> 22:15.806 align:middle
in vaccine rollouts and
funding for distribution

22:16.126 --> 22:18.696 align:middle
in your forecasts and what
that timeline looks like.

22:18.696 --> 22:21.446 align:middle
And then, secondly,
I'm wondering if you,

22:21.446 --> 22:23.896 align:middle
yourself haven't-has
been-have been vaccinated,

22:24.136 --> 22:25.666 align:middle
along with other FOMC members.

22:25.856 --> 22:26.276 align:middle
Thank you.

22:27.136 --> 22:28.106 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

22:28.956 --> 22:34.536 align:middle
So, in terms of the
rollout of the vaccines,

22:34.536 --> 22:36.446 align:middle
we see what everyone
else sees, right?

22:36.446 --> 22:40.596 align:middle
We see that we're, we're
vaccinating people and at a rate

22:40.596 --> 22:42.086 align:middle
of about a million
a day, apparently,

22:42.086 --> 22:46.056 align:middle
and that it'll take quite
a while to get-to get

22:46.116 --> 22:48.446 align:middle
to the numbers that the
experts say are required

22:48.446 --> 22:49.416 align:middle
to get to herd immunity.

22:50.296 --> 22:53.806 align:middle
And we think it's
going to be a struggle.

22:54.446 --> 23:01.076 align:middle
You'll notice that we, we said
that the, the pandemic still,

23:01.266 --> 23:08.196 align:middle
still provides considerable
downside risks to the economy.

23:08.196 --> 23:10.456 align:middle
And that's one of
the reasons why,

23:10.456 --> 23:12.516 align:middle
is that-is the slowness
of the rollout.

23:12.516 --> 23:15.456 align:middle
The other-another reason
why is just the arrival

23:15.456 --> 23:16.556 align:middle
of these new virus strains.

23:16.556 --> 23:18.726 align:middle
We don't know how to-we
don't know how to model that.

23:18.726 --> 23:19.966 align:middle
You know, we can
have a base case,

23:19.966 --> 23:25.756 align:middle
but we realize no one knows how
the-how this new vaccine will

23:25.976 --> 23:28.846 align:middle
roll out, how successful it
will be, how high we'll be able

23:28.846 --> 23:31.986 align:middle
to drive vaccination, and
those sorts of things.

23:31.986 --> 23:33.556 align:middle
So we have a base case,

23:33.646 --> 23:35.856 align:middle
and we-but we always-we
always look at,

23:36.466 --> 23:37.996 align:middle
at the range of possibilities.

23:37.996 --> 23:40.286 align:middle
In this case, we particularly
look at the downside risks.

23:40.346 --> 23:44.016 align:middle
You know, that's really what
we do is, we, we set policies

23:44.016 --> 23:46.176 align:middle
so that we're, we're, you know,

23:46.176 --> 23:47.566 align:middle
we're going to remain
accommodative

23:47.566 --> 23:49.936 align:middle
until we actually see
improvement in the economy

23:49.936 --> 23:53.516 align:middle
and not just in the
outlook, in the data.

23:53.516 --> 23:55.966 align:middle
So that's, that's how we
think about, about that.

23:56.106 --> 23:59.346 align:middle
And I would-I would also add,
there's nothing more important

23:59.346 --> 24:01.936 align:middle
to the economy now than, than
people getting vaccinated.

24:02.376 --> 24:05.406 align:middle
You know, if you-if you,
you think about the places

24:05.406 --> 24:09.086 align:middle
where the economy is weak-I
mentioned bars and restaurants.

24:09.086 --> 24:13.056 align:middle
That's 400,000 jobs we lost
last month, and that's all

24:13.576 --> 24:15.186 align:middle
because of the spread
of the pandemic.

24:15.446 --> 24:17.296 align:middle
Many other areas of
the economy-actually,

24:17.296 --> 24:20.226 align:middle
there was actually job creation
in-you know, in goods production

24:20.226 --> 24:22.496 align:middle
and in some service
parts-industries as well.

24:23.126 --> 24:26.816 align:middle
But we're just not going to
be able to get that last group

24:26.816 --> 24:28.616 align:middle
of people back to
work-and it's a big group

24:28.616 --> 24:31.686 align:middle
of people-until we get
the pandemic behind us.

24:31.686 --> 24:34.356 align:middle
We, we have not won this yet.

24:34.356 --> 24:36.186 align:middle
We haven't succeeded
in doing this yet,

24:36.186 --> 24:37.646 align:middle
and we need to stay
focused on it

24:37.716 --> 24:39.806 align:middle
as a country and, and get there.

24:39.806 --> 24:41.646 align:middle
And, you know, we clearly
can, but we're going

24:41.646 --> 24:42.796 align:middle
to have to stay focused.

24:42.796 --> 24:45.286 align:middle
And that includes-you
know, that includes us

24:45.286 --> 24:46.556 align:middle
at the Fed, monetary policy.

24:47.296 --> 24:50.206 align:middle
I, I have been vaccinated
once, and I expect

24:50.206 --> 24:52.226 align:middle
to get my second
vaccine sometime soon.

24:52.226 --> 24:52.406 align:middle
Thank you.

24:52.666 --> 24:54.816 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

24:54.816 --> 24:55.096 align:middle
Thank you.

24:55.236 --> 24:55.796 align:middle
Michael McKee.

24:56.066 --> 24:56.726 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

24:57.346 --> 25:03.476 align:middle
A question-I have a question
that I was going to ask,

25:03.476 --> 25:06.916 align:middle
but I need to follow up for
a second on the questions

25:06.916 --> 25:10.746 align:middle
about the markets and
macroprudential, and that is,

25:10.786 --> 25:12.936 align:middle
you have one tool you can use.

25:12.936 --> 25:17.516 align:middle
Would you be discussing-have
you discussed raising margin

25:17.516 --> 25:19.566 align:middle
requirements under Regulation T?

25:19.566 --> 25:20.596 align:middle
And, if not, why not?

25:21.846 --> 25:22.096 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

25:22.146 --> 25:23.476 align:middle
No, we haven't-we
haven't done that.

25:23.476 --> 25:26.356 align:middle
I mean, remember, we're, we're
focused on maximum employment,

25:26.426 --> 25:28.926 align:middle
price stability,
financial stability

25:28.926 --> 25:31.566 align:middle
as I defined it-the
broad financial sector.

25:32.176 --> 25:36.636 align:middle
And, and that-that's not,
you know-over the years, we,

25:36.666 --> 25:38.966 align:middle
we consult the fact that
we have that authority.

25:38.966 --> 25:40.676 align:middle
But, no, it's not
something we're looking

25:40.676 --> 25:41.566 align:middle
at right now at all.

25:42.966 --> 25:43.356 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

25:43.356 --> 25:43.586 align:middle
All right.

25:43.586 --> 25:46.916 align:middle
My follow-up question is, a lot
of people in the markets think

25:46.916 --> 25:48.826 align:middle
that you're basically
stuck right now,

25:48.826 --> 25:51.286 align:middle
because you can't really go
lower with the zero bound,

25:51.396 --> 25:53.416 align:middle
rejecting negative
interest rates,

25:53.416 --> 25:54.656 align:middle
and you can't really go higher

25:54.656 --> 25:57.226 align:middle
because of the threat
of a taper tantrum.

25:57.226 --> 26:00.606 align:middle
Is the Fed locked into a
very narrow corridor now?

26:00.986 --> 26:04.786 align:middle
And, if not, you did say
you would signal any change

26:04.786 --> 26:07.066 align:middle
in interest rates
a long time ahead.

26:07.486 --> 26:09.216 align:middle
But [former] New York Fed
President Bill Dudley says

26:09.216 --> 26:11.216 align:middle
there's no way you can
avoid a taper tantrum.

26:11.216 --> 26:12.386 align:middle
So how do you do that?

26:13.026 --> 26:13.846 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

26:13.846 --> 26:17.636 align:middle
Well, first, I-we, we think our
policy stance is, is just right.

26:17.636 --> 26:21.126 align:middle
We think it's providing
significant support

26:21.126 --> 26:22.846 align:middle
for economic activity
and hiring.

26:23.396 --> 26:28.316 align:middle
We, you know, we, we adopted a
new monetary policy framework

26:28.316 --> 26:30.636 align:middle
of flexible average
inflation targeting in August.

26:31.046 --> 26:34.266 align:middle
In September, we, we
implemented rate guidance that,

26:34.366 --> 26:37.836 align:middle
that was consistent with and
based on that new framework.

26:38.036 --> 26:40.306 align:middle
In December, we did the
same for asset purchases.

26:40.306 --> 26:44.496 align:middle
So we now have, you know,
strong guidance on, on rates

26:44.496 --> 26:48.096 align:middle
and on asset purchases that's
providing very strong support

26:48.096 --> 26:49.216 align:middle
for economic activity.

26:49.216 --> 26:51.176 align:middle
If you look at the
sectors of the economy

26:51.176 --> 26:54.396 align:middle
that are interest sensitive, you
will see very strong activity:

26:54.636 --> 26:58.876 align:middle
housing, durable
goods, automobile sales.

26:59.206 --> 27:01.436 align:middle
So, you know, our, our
policies are working.

27:01.436 --> 27:04.166 align:middle
And we think that, that they
are-we think our policy stance

27:04.166 --> 27:04.976 align:middle
is, is right.

27:05.546 --> 27:07.566 align:middle
That said, there's clearly
more that we can do.

27:07.666 --> 27:09.746 align:middle
With asset purchases,
for example, we can,

27:09.886 --> 27:13.126 align:middle
you know-that's, that's a tool
we can do more-we can strengthen

27:13.126 --> 27:14.626 align:middle
our guidance, too,
if we were to think

27:14.976 --> 27:16.236 align:middle
that that were appropriate.

27:16.236 --> 27:17.516 align:middle
What you see is an economy

27:17.516 --> 27:20.496 align:middle
where what's holding it
back is not the lack of,

27:20.706 --> 27:22.506 align:middle
of policy support from the Fed.

27:22.506 --> 27:24.186 align:middle
It's the pandemic.

27:24.426 --> 27:26.226 align:middle
It's, it's the spread
of the disease

27:26.296 --> 27:30.396 align:middle
and people's reluctance
or, or inability to partake

27:30.396 --> 27:32.996 align:middle
in certain kinds of economic
activities, which amount to a,

27:33.296 --> 27:36.406 align:middle
a meaningful part
of the economy.

27:36.866 --> 27:39.346 align:middle
So that's, that's
what I would say.

27:39.346 --> 27:40.936 align:middle
We certainly have
things that we can do,

27:40.936 --> 27:42.906 align:middle
but we think we're-we think
we're in the right place.

27:43.406 --> 27:44.686 align:middle
You know, in terms of-in terms

27:44.866 --> 27:47.616 align:middle
of tapering, it's
just premature.

27:47.616 --> 27:49.766 align:middle
We just created the,
the guidance.

27:50.246 --> 27:50.916 align:middle
We said we'd want

27:50.916 --> 27:54.436 align:middle
to see substantial further
progress toward our goals before

27:54.436 --> 27:58.646 align:middle
we modify our, our
asset purchase guidance.

27:59.186 --> 28:01.866 align:middle
It's just too early to
be talking about dates,

28:01.866 --> 28:04.516 align:middle
which we should be
focused on, on progress.

28:04.516 --> 28:06.086 align:middle
We'll need to see
actual progress.

28:06.136 --> 28:09.146 align:middle
And when, when we see
ourselves getting to that point,

28:09.146 --> 28:12.866 align:middle
we'll communicate clearly
about it to, to the public

28:13.056 --> 28:16.716 align:middle
so nobody will be surprised
when the time comes.

28:16.716 --> 28:18.116 align:middle
And we'll do that
well in advance

28:18.116 --> 28:22.046 align:middle
of actually considering what
will be a pretty gradual taper.

28:23.156 --> 28:23.996 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

28:23.996 --> 28:26.646 align:middle
Well, if I might, you-your
policies are working,

28:26.646 --> 28:27.926 align:middle
and you can maybe do more.

28:27.926 --> 28:31.166 align:middle
But the question is, can you
stop doing it when it's time?

28:31.586 --> 28:32.736 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

28:32.896 --> 28:34.036 align:middle
Yes. So, you know, I,

28:34.036 --> 28:38.206 align:middle
I was here-we had all the same
questions back in-after the

28:38.206 --> 28:39.856 align:middle
Global Financial Crisis.

28:40.166 --> 28:41.246 align:middle
We raised interest rates.

28:41.996 --> 28:43.516 align:middle
We froze the balance sheet size,

28:43.516 --> 28:45.146 align:middle
and then we shrank the
balance sheet size.

28:45.946 --> 28:48.176 align:middle
So there's no reason why we, we
won't be able to do that again.

28:48.176 --> 28:51.236 align:middle
In fact, we learned a
lot from that experience.

28:51.536 --> 28:52.676 align:middle
And, you know, we,

28:52.676 --> 28:54.316 align:middle
we understand-as
we understood then,

28:54.316 --> 28:57.296 align:middle
but even more so-we
understand that the-that the way

28:57.296 --> 28:59.796 align:middle
to do it is to communicate
well in advance, to,

28:59.796 --> 29:03.086 align:middle
to do predictable things,
and to move gradually.

29:03.086 --> 29:04.196 align:middle
And that's, that's
what we're going to do.

29:04.196 --> 29:05.456 align:middle
We're going to be
very transparent.

29:05.956 --> 29:09.306 align:middle
But, honestly, no-you
know, the whole focus

29:09.306 --> 29:11.616 align:middle
on exit is premature,
if I may say.

29:12.016 --> 29:15.286 align:middle
We're focused on finishing
the job we're doing,

29:15.336 --> 29:16.666 align:middle
which is to support the economy,

29:16.666 --> 29:18.726 align:middle
to give the economy
the support it needs.

29:18.766 --> 29:21.126 align:middle
There are people out there
who've lost their jobs.

29:21.566 --> 29:24.236 align:middle
It's essential that we get
them back to work as quickly

29:24.236 --> 29:27.686 align:middle
as possible, and we want to do
everything we can to do that.

29:27.686 --> 29:29.846 align:middle
And that is our primary
focus right now.

29:29.846 --> 29:33.846 align:middle
It's, it's, it's too soon to
be-to be worried about that.

29:33.846 --> 29:35.356 align:middle
You know, when we come to exit,

29:35.356 --> 29:37.866 align:middle
we have a-we have an
understanding of how to do that,

29:37.866 --> 29:39.056 align:middle
and we'll do it very carefully.

29:39.056 --> 29:40.516 align:middle
But in the meantime,
our focus is

29:40.516 --> 29:42.406 align:middle
on giving the economy
the support it needs.

29:42.596 --> 29:43.026 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

29:44.806 --> 29:45.396 align:middle
Thank you.

29:45.396 --> 29:46.206 align:middle
Victoria Guida.

29:47.366 --> 29:48.156 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

29:48.156 --> 29:48.906 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

29:48.906 --> 29:52.486 align:middle
I just wanted to go back to
fiscal stimulus for a second.

29:52.486 --> 29:54.716 align:middle
You know, we just had
a $900 billion package,

29:54.716 --> 29:57.186 align:middle
and now Congress is
talking about doing more.

29:57.276 --> 30:03.326 align:middle
Do you expect more aid directly
to consumers to be inflationary?

30:03.326 --> 30:06.796 align:middle
And, specifically, how worried
should lawmakers be about,

30:06.796 --> 30:09.246 align:middle
you know, causing concerning
levels of inflation?

30:11.196 --> 30:11.596 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

30:11.596 --> 30:18.216 align:middle
I, I would say that, again,
we have been struggling

30:18.216 --> 30:20.876 align:middle
with disinflationary
forces for some time.

30:21.386 --> 30:24.296 align:middle
If you look around the
world-look in Western Europe,

30:24.506 --> 30:29.856 align:middle
look at Japan-around the
world, large economies have,

30:30.046 --> 30:32.946 align:middle
have felt much more downward
pressure on inflation,

30:32.946 --> 30:36.246 align:middle
have fallen short of
their inflation goals,

30:36.296 --> 30:37.406 align:middle
for some time now.

30:37.986 --> 30:41.876 align:middle
That is the broad global
macroeconomic context

30:41.876 --> 30:42.686 align:middle
that we all live in.

30:42.686 --> 30:46.796 align:middle
And we believe that those global
forces-which are, you know,

30:46.796 --> 30:49.306 align:middle
aging demographics,
advancing technology,

30:49.506 --> 30:53.006 align:middle
and globalization-those, those
forces are still in effect.

30:53.006 --> 30:54.946 align:middle
Now, they may slow
down, and, and it's,

30:54.946 --> 30:57.376 align:middle
it's an interesting
question, the extent to which,

30:57.376 --> 31:00.436 align:middle
for example, globalization
may slow down or even reverse.

31:01.076 --> 31:05.666 align:middle
Notwithstanding that,
inflation dynamics

31:05.666 --> 31:07.956 align:middle
in the United States
have, have consisted

31:07.956 --> 31:10.246 align:middle
of a flat Phillips curve
and low persistence for,

31:10.326 --> 31:11.426 align:middle
for a long time.

31:11.426 --> 31:14.256 align:middle
We do not expect
in the near term

31:14.256 --> 31:15.886 align:middle
that that will change
significantly.

31:15.886 --> 31:19.246 align:middle
It may evolve over time,
but significant time.

31:19.246 --> 31:22.646 align:middle
You know, these things are-these
things are, are changing,

31:22.646 --> 31:24.336 align:middle
but they don't change
at a rapid rate.

31:24.336 --> 31:26.706 align:middle
And there's-I, I don't
believe that, that they will.

31:26.906 --> 31:30.356 align:middle
So I wouldn't-I'm, I'm
not-I'm much more worried

31:30.356 --> 31:33.736 align:middle
about falling short
of a complete recovery

31:34.326 --> 31:38.836 align:middle
and losing people's careers and
lives that they-that they built

31:39.496 --> 31:41.926 align:middle
because they don't get
to work-back to work

31:41.926 --> 31:43.216 align:middle
in time and things like that.

31:43.216 --> 31:45.886 align:middle
I'm more concerned about
that and the damage

31:45.886 --> 31:47.426 align:middle
that will do not
just to their lives,

31:47.496 --> 31:49.596 align:middle
but to the United
States economy,

31:49.596 --> 31:51.476 align:middle
to the productive
capacity of the economy.

31:51.746 --> 31:55.066 align:middle
I'm more concerned about that
than about the possibility,

31:55.836 --> 31:57.946 align:middle
which exists, of
higher inflation.

31:58.156 --> 32:01.216 align:middle
Frankly, we welcome
slightly higher inflation,

32:01.216 --> 32:02.246 align:middle
somewhat higher inflation.

32:02.706 --> 32:05.626 align:middle
The kind of troubling inflation
that people like me grew

32:05.626 --> 32:09.386 align:middle
up with seems, seems far
away and unlikely in,

32:09.386 --> 32:11.346 align:middle
in the both domestic
and global context

32:11.346 --> 32:12.696 align:middle
that we've been in
for some time.

32:13.036 --> 32:13.316 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

32:15.946 --> 32:16.406 align:middle
Thank you.

32:16.406 --> 32:17.976 align:middle
Steve Matthews, Bloomberg.

32:21.166 --> 32:22.036 align:middle
STEVE MATTHEWS.

32:22.116 --> 32:25.576 align:middle
Chair Powell, Treasury
Secretary Yellen,

32:25.976 --> 32:29.906 align:middle
in a memo to her
employees yesterday,

32:30.146 --> 32:32.466 align:middle
spoke of the close
working relationship

32:32.646 --> 32:34.416 align:middle
with the Federal Reserve.

32:35.056 --> 32:37.916 align:middle
And I'm wondering how you would
describe the relationship with,

32:38.066 --> 32:42.706 align:middle
with Treasury under Chair
Yellen, if you've met with,

32:43.166 --> 32:46.626 align:middle
with, with Treasury
Secretary Yellen and,

32:47.316 --> 32:52.956 align:middle
and/or President Biden
since he has taken office.

32:52.956 --> 32:59.836 align:middle
And, you know, secondly,
about that, as you know,

33:00.066 --> 33:03.936 align:middle
in the last four years,
there was constant criticism

33:03.986 --> 33:07.386 align:middle
from the-of the Fed
from the President

33:07.386 --> 33:11.256 align:middle
and from other officials
of the Administration.

33:11.256 --> 33:15.036 align:middle
Do you have any kind of
assurance or expectation

33:15.516 --> 33:21.156 align:middle
that this will now be more
of a hands-off attitude,

33:21.156 --> 33:24.036 align:middle
in terms of commenting
on monetary policy

33:24.036 --> 33:25.316 align:middle
from the Administration?

33:27.056 --> 33:27.446 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

33:27.446 --> 33:29.646 align:middle
Okay. So I have the
highest respect

33:29.646 --> 33:31.536 align:middle
and admiration for
Secretary Yellen.

33:32.626 --> 33:34.826 align:middle
And I'm, I'm sure that
we're going to have a,

33:34.826 --> 33:36.286 align:middle
a good working relationship
together.

33:36.586 --> 33:38.336 align:middle
Absolutely sure.

33:38.596 --> 33:40.556 align:middle
We'll also have a good
institutional relationship

33:40.556 --> 33:41.866 align:middle
between the Fed and
the Treasury.

33:41.866 --> 33:44.196 align:middle
And I expect that
that'll be very good and,

33:44.196 --> 33:45.056 align:middle
and very productive.

33:45.706 --> 33:46.726 align:middle
It'll be collaborative.

33:46.726 --> 33:47.576 align:middle
We'll work together.

33:47.956 --> 33:51.206 align:middle
Now, the way that works
is, is that, you know,

33:51.206 --> 33:53.986 align:middle
we know-the agencies know each
other well, and this is true

33:53.986 --> 33:56.756 align:middle
of finance ministries and
central banks around the world.

33:57.206 --> 33:58.406 align:middle
We know to stay in our lanes.

33:58.406 --> 33:59.756 align:middle
We know we have different
authorities.

33:59.856 --> 34:03.186 align:middle
We know that we work together
on some things to-you know,

34:03.296 --> 34:04.486 align:middle
for the benefit of the public.

34:04.486 --> 34:08.216 align:middle
And I'm, I'm absolutely sure
that we're going to do that.

34:08.216 --> 34:10.416 align:middle
I, you know, haven't-I
haven't spoken to,

34:10.616 --> 34:13.326 align:middle
to Secretary Yellen-I'm
going to be calling her

34:13.326 --> 34:15.706 align:middle
"Chair Yellen" most of the time,
so you just have to be patient

34:15.706 --> 34:17.606 align:middle
with me-Secretary Yellen.

34:17.606 --> 34:20.106 align:middle
I haven't spoken to her
since I congratulated her

34:20.106 --> 34:21.016 align:middle
on being nominated.

34:21.346 --> 34:26.366 align:middle
I do expect very soon to begin
our regular calls or-and,

34:26.366 --> 34:28.916 align:middle
and ultimately meetings,
which have gone on, really,

34:28.916 --> 34:31.006 align:middle
for, you know, 70 years.

34:31.006 --> 34:32.346 align:middle
The Treasury Secretary and the,

34:32.346 --> 34:35.656 align:middle
and the Fed Chair have had
weekly or so meetings, lunches,

34:35.706 --> 34:37.916 align:middle
breakfast, calls,
depending on the situation.

34:37.916 --> 34:41.076 align:middle
So I expect that
will-that will happen soon.

34:41.106 --> 34:46.036 align:middle
I've-I have not met the, the
President, President Biden.

34:46.476 --> 34:50.726 align:middle
And just, you know, I, I, I
don't-I don't have any comment

34:50.726 --> 34:52.016 align:middle
on your-on your last question.

34:52.136 --> 34:55.506 align:middle
I wouldn't want to comment
one way or the other.

34:56.336 --> 34:56.996 align:middle
STEVE MATTHEWS.

34:56.996 --> 34:58.096 align:middle
Just to follow up briefly,

34:58.096 --> 35:02.846 align:middle
you have some 13(3)
emergency programs

35:02.846 --> 35:05.596 align:middle
that will expire
March 31 that the Fed

35:05.596 --> 35:07.526 align:middle
and the Treasury have
worked on together.

35:07.906 --> 35:12.096 align:middle
Do you have an expectation of
whether those will be left,

35:12.096 --> 35:15.046 align:middle
left to expire then or
whether they will be extended?

35:16.216 --> 35:16.546 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

35:16.546 --> 35:18.816 align:middle
So I-I'll just say that I
think our facilities were very

35:18.816 --> 35:21.546 align:middle
successful in supporting the
economy during its darkest

35:21.546 --> 35:24.816 align:middle
moments and I believe
saved-protected the loss

35:24.816 --> 35:26.316 align:middle
of millions of jobs.

35:26.896 --> 35:29.796 align:middle
We're going to continue
to monitor financial

35:29.796 --> 35:31.656 align:middle
and credit conditions
throughout the economy.

35:31.656 --> 35:34.306 align:middle
And, and if the kind of
emergency conditions arise

35:34.306 --> 35:35.676 align:middle
that are required
under that law,

35:36.106 --> 35:38.866 align:middle
then our emergency lending
tools will remain available.

35:39.316 --> 35:43.826 align:middle
Now, I've had no discussions
with anybody at Treasury

35:44.466 --> 35:47.906 align:middle
on that, because I haven't had
any discussions with anybody

35:47.906 --> 35:50.386 align:middle
at Treasury other than meet-you
know, high-level meetings

35:50.386 --> 35:54.816 align:middle
with the-with the transition
team a month or so ago.

35:56.396 --> 35:59.136 align:middle
And once we start having those,
those meetings, of course,

35:59.496 --> 36:01.996 align:middle
I'll observe, you know,
the long-standing practice

36:01.996 --> 36:04.766 align:middle
of not talking about
confidential discussions.

36:04.816 --> 36:06.626 align:middle
But as of now, there haven't
been any discussions at all.

36:06.746 --> 36:07.256 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

36:08.946 --> 36:09.556 align:middle
Thank you.

36:09.556 --> 36:10.846 align:middle
Howard Schneider, Reuters.

36:12.746 --> 36:13.356 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

36:13.356 --> 36:14.816 align:middle
Thanks, Chair Powell,
for doing this.

36:15.086 --> 36:17.066 align:middle
So a couple questions on timing.

36:17.066 --> 36:17.986 align:middle
In the statement,

36:17.986 --> 36:21.556 align:middle
you removed-regarding the
coronavirus-you removed the time

36:21.556 --> 36:25.476 align:middle
reference "short and medium
term" when it comes to the risks

36:25.476 --> 36:27.916 align:middle
to the economic outlook,
and it simply now says

36:27.986 --> 36:29.896 align:middle
"considerable risks to
the economic outlook."

36:30.176 --> 36:35.296 align:middle
Should we read that as a sort
of a positive change that you,

36:35.336 --> 36:38.516 align:middle
you now see an end-sort of the
endgame of this down the road?

36:38.916 --> 36:40.236 align:middle
Or is it more that you're,

36:40.236 --> 36:43.396 align:middle
you're worried it could
last longer than you expect?

36:43.396 --> 36:46.296 align:middle
And a related issue, have
you given any guidance

36:46.296 --> 36:48.356 align:middle
yet to the Fed staff or System

36:48.356 --> 36:52.216 align:middle
on when the Fed itself might
resume in-person events?

36:52.216 --> 36:53.696 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

36:53.846 --> 36:56.346 align:middle
Yes. So in the statement,
on the language,

36:57.016 --> 37:00.856 align:middle
we dropped "in the medium term,"
because, really, the risks are,

37:00.896 --> 37:02.566 align:middle
are in the near term, frankly.

37:02.636 --> 37:05.336 align:middle
You know, as I mentioned, it's
the rollout of the vaccine.

37:05.476 --> 37:09.246 align:middle
It's the arrival of new strains
that, that are more contagious

37:09.246 --> 37:10.446 align:middle
and perhaps more virulent.

37:10.896 --> 37:12.956 align:middle
And those are the-those
are the-and also just the

37:12.956 --> 37:13.976 align:middle
ongoing-third thing, of course,

37:13.976 --> 37:15.346 align:middle
is the ongoing spread
of the virus.

37:15.346 --> 37:16.456 align:middle
It's, it's in the near term.

37:16.456 --> 37:17.786 align:middle
It's not in the medium term.

37:18.156 --> 37:19.996 align:middle
We were thinking-when we
were thinking "medium term,"

37:19.996 --> 37:23.046 align:middle
we were thinking of longer-term
scarring and things like that.

37:23.606 --> 37:26.446 align:middle
Nonetheless, I mean, I
think what-to go to your,

37:26.526 --> 37:29.206 align:middle
your second question,
and as I mentioned in my,

37:29.206 --> 37:33.926 align:middle
in my opening remarks-there
is good evidence to,

37:33.926 --> 37:38.476 align:middle
to support a stronger economy
in the second half of this year.

37:38.476 --> 37:41.806 align:middle
In fact, if you look at,
at-as we do-look at a range

37:41.806 --> 37:46.226 align:middle
of private forecasters, what was
their forecast in, in December,

37:46.226 --> 37:47.456 align:middle
and what's their forecast now?

37:47.456 --> 37:48.456 align:middle
Right across the board:

37:49.176 --> 37:51.836 align:middle
much higher forecasts
for 2021 growth.

37:51.836 --> 37:54.206 align:middle
And that's because of the
ongoing rollout of the vaccines

37:54.206 --> 37:56.766 align:middle
and also because of fiscal
policy, expectations,

37:56.766 --> 38:00.406 align:middle
and the reality of, of the CRRA
Act [Consolidated Appropriations

38:00.956 --> 38:01.446 align:middle
Act, 2021] getting done.

38:01.446 --> 38:03.696 align:middle
So there is-there is
a positive case there.

38:03.856 --> 38:07.266 align:middle
But that-think of that
as-the sort of base case is,

38:07.266 --> 38:09.566 align:middle
is a strong economy in the
second half of the year.

38:09.956 --> 38:15.576 align:middle
The language says that there's
still-I forget the exact

38:15.576 --> 38:17.646 align:middle
language-downside risks.

38:17.746 --> 38:21.046 align:middle
We used an adjective.

38:22.416 --> 38:22.946 align:middle
It was

38:22.946 --> 38:25.806 align:middle
"considerable"-"considerable
risks to the economic outlook."

38:25.806 --> 38:29.136 align:middle
So there are considerable risks,
risks to the economic outlook.

38:29.816 --> 38:32.426 align:middle
Nonetheless, that is-that
is a more positive outlook.

38:32.706 --> 38:33.646 align:middle
And that's really how,

38:33.646 --> 38:35.366 align:middle
how I would-I would
parse that for you.

38:36.036 --> 38:38.526 align:middle
I don't know when we're going
to come back to work, and I'm,

38:38.526 --> 38:40.446 align:middle
I'm actually in the
Eccles Building here today.

38:40.446 --> 38:42.186 align:middle
And I don't know
when that'll be.

38:42.376 --> 38:46.856 align:middle
I mean, we-I can't wait to
be working in person again.

38:46.856 --> 38:48.406 align:middle
But, you know, we-we've
been able

38:48.406 --> 38:50.086 align:middle
to work successfully remotely.

38:50.506 --> 38:51.406 align:middle
We really have.

38:51.406 --> 38:53.686 align:middle
And, you know, we're
not going to push people

38:53.726 --> 38:54.626 align:middle
when they're uncomfortable.

38:54.626 --> 38:56.216 align:middle
We're going to wait till
people are comfortable

38:56.936 --> 39:00.266 align:middle
and wait till it's
well and truly time

39:01.046 --> 39:02.496 align:middle
to get back together in person.

39:03.016 --> 39:04.466 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

39:04.466 --> 39:04.826 align:middle
Thank you.

39:04.826 --> 39:05.446 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

39:05.626 --> 39:06.266 align:middle
Thank you.

39:06.266 --> 39:07.106 align:middle
Hannah Lang.

39:11.356 --> 39:12.176 align:middle
HANNAH LANG.

39:12.176 --> 39:13.236 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

39:13.236 --> 39:15.146 align:middle
Thank you so much for
taking our questions.

39:15.146 --> 39:17.446 align:middle
The Fed in December
eased its restrictions

39:17.446 --> 39:19.866 align:middle
on bank dividend payments
and share repurchases.

39:19.866 --> 39:22.926 align:middle
And so I wanted to ask what
factors the Fed will be looking

39:22.926 --> 39:25.286 align:middle
at to determine what
level banks can pay

39:25.286 --> 39:26.366 align:middle
out in the second quarter

39:26.366 --> 39:28.636 align:middle
and when we can expect
such a decision.

39:28.636 --> 39:30.086 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

39:30.086 --> 39:34.106 align:middle
Okay. So we're, we're monitoring
that on an ongoing basis,

39:34.106 --> 39:39.076 align:middle
continue to-continuing to
evaluate our, our restrictions.

39:39.076 --> 39:41.766 align:middle
And we haven't made a decision
about whether to continue them

39:42.256 --> 39:43.486 align:middle
in the second quarter or not.

39:43.576 --> 39:45.376 align:middle
We're just going to look
at bank-we're going to look

39:45.376 --> 39:47.916 align:middle
at the whole range
of, of information,

39:47.916 --> 39:51.756 align:middle
including economic activity,
banking activity, the,

39:51.876 --> 39:53.986 align:middle
the success in vaccination.

39:53.986 --> 39:56.486 align:middle
All of those things will
go into our assessment

39:56.486 --> 39:58.986 align:middle
of what the right answer
is to that question.

39:59.076 --> 40:01.336 align:middle
I think we've been
careful about,

40:01.536 --> 40:03.816 align:middle
about rolling back
those restrictions.

40:03.816 --> 40:05.736 align:middle
And, and I'm pleased
with where we are.

40:05.736 --> 40:08.356 align:middle
Let's remember that the
banks-that the banks

40:08.356 --> 40:10.486 align:middle
that are subject to the
stress tests have taken very,

40:10.486 --> 40:13.046 align:middle
very large reserves-very
large loss reserves,

40:13.146 --> 40:15.676 align:middle
and also increased
their capital.

40:15.916 --> 40:18.396 align:middle
They've actually had-they
have higher capital ratios now

40:18.396 --> 40:20.116 align:middle
than they had at the
beginning of the pandemic.

40:20.586 --> 40:23.856 align:middle
So the banking system
has held up well here.

40:24.316 --> 40:27.736 align:middle
And, and-but, you know,
we're going to be careful

40:27.736 --> 40:31.106 align:middle
about this and, you
know, move based on data,

40:31.816 --> 40:34.036 align:middle
and all of the data, when
we make that decision.

40:34.036 --> 40:34.666 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

40:38.916 --> 40:39.476 align:middle
Thank you.

40:39.476 --> 40:39.806 align:middle
Edward Lawrence.

40:39.806 --> 40:41.316 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

40:44.486 --> 40:46.606 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, thank you
for taking the question.

40:46.606 --> 40:50.766 align:middle
So I'm interested in
the housing sector.

40:50.766 --> 40:54.006 align:middle
Home prices are rising,
9 percent in some areas,

40:54.006 --> 40:55.996 align:middle
because of low interest rates.

40:55.996 --> 40:59.136 align:middle
Are you concerned about a
bubble forming there yet?

40:59.356 --> 41:02.366 align:middle
And is there a price increase,
you know, that you're looking

41:02.366 --> 41:04.026 align:middle
at where it might
change the level

41:04.026 --> 41:05.736 align:middle
of mortgage-backed
securities the Fed is buying?

41:05.736 --> 41:08.786 align:middle
And, as a continuation
of that, is the bubble

41:08.786 --> 41:12.386 align:middle
in the corporate debt cycle
more concerning for you?

41:13.246 --> 41:13.956 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

41:14.096 --> 41:17.886 align:middle
So, on housing, you know,
housing is now-the level

41:17.886 --> 41:20.756 align:middle
of housing activity is
at its highest level

41:20.756 --> 41:22.416 align:middle
since before the
Global Financial Crisis,

41:22.416 --> 41:23.186 align:middle
in some measures.

41:23.186 --> 41:25.886 align:middle
So we've had a very
strong rebound in housing.

41:26.286 --> 41:30.046 align:middle
Some of the, the tightness
in housing markets,

41:30.686 --> 41:32.036 align:middle
we think-which has led to the,

41:32.036 --> 41:34.636 align:middle
the significant price
increases this year-we think is,

41:34.636 --> 41:35.876 align:middle
is a passing phenomenon.

41:35.876 --> 41:37.486 align:middle
There was a lot of
pent-up demand.

41:38.016 --> 41:41.556 align:middle
There's a one-time thing
happening with people

41:41.556 --> 41:43.736 align:middle
who are spending all of
their time in their house,

41:43.736 --> 41:46.296 align:middle
and they're thinking either
"I need a bigger house"

41:46.296 --> 41:47.376 align:middle
or "I need another house

41:47.446 --> 41:50.806 align:middle
and a different house-or a
second house," in some cases.

41:50.806 --> 41:52.866 align:middle
So there's just-there's
a-there's a one-time shift

41:52.866 --> 41:55.776 align:middle
in demand that we think
will get satisfied-also,

41:55.776 --> 41:57.256 align:middle
that will call forth supply.

41:57.726 --> 41:59.656 align:middle
And that will-that
will, we think-we think

41:59.656 --> 42:03.346 align:middle
that those price increases are
unlikely to be sustained for,

42:03.346 --> 42:05.756 align:middle
for all of those reasons.

42:05.906 --> 42:09.366 align:middle
So you asked about
corporate debt, I guess?

42:09.916 --> 42:10.526 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

42:10.706 --> 42:11.516 align:middle
Yes. I'm just concerned

42:11.516 --> 42:13.296 align:middle
if that's a bubble
that you're watching.

42:13.386 --> 42:14.426 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

42:14.426 --> 42:16.966 align:middle
So we, we monitor
pretty much all

42:16.966 --> 42:18.866 align:middle
of the big financial
markets pretty carefully.

42:18.866 --> 42:20.316 align:middle
And, you know, what's
happened in,

42:20.846 --> 42:25.186 align:middle
in the corporate debt market,
beginning with the announcement

42:25.186 --> 42:27.066 align:middle
of our corporate credit
facility, has been,

42:27.666 --> 42:30.596 align:middle
you've seen lenders lending
and borrowers borrowing.

42:30.746 --> 42:35.486 align:middle
You've seen relatively
significantly fewer defaults

42:36.886 --> 42:38.246 align:middle
than we-than we expected.

42:38.246 --> 42:40.036 align:middle
There were a lot of
downgrades and some defaults

42:40.036 --> 42:42.096 align:middle
at the beginning, and those
have really slowed down.

42:42.386 --> 42:44.486 align:middle
And, by the way, the same
is true for bank loans.

42:44.516 --> 42:48.476 align:middle
Banks are not experiencing
the kinds of defaults that,

42:48.476 --> 42:49.736 align:middle
that we all were concerned

42:49.736 --> 42:51.796 align:middle
about in the early
months of the pandemic.

42:51.796 --> 42:53.246 align:middle
It's just not materializing.

42:53.246 --> 42:54.326 align:middle
So they're having
to reverse some

42:54.326 --> 42:56.446 align:middle
of their loss reserves,
actually.

42:56.926 --> 43:01.626 align:middle
Debts-corporate debt
spreads are, are tight.

43:01.626 --> 43:02.326 align:middle
They've tightened.

43:02.326 --> 43:04.686 align:middle
They were very broad-high-wide,
of course,

43:04.686 --> 43:07.476 align:middle
at the-during the acute
phase of the pandemic,

43:07.476 --> 43:10.546 align:middle
and they've-you know,
they're now at the lower end

43:10.546 --> 43:11.886 align:middle
of their typical range.

43:11.886 --> 43:14.866 align:middle
And we do monitor that.

43:14.966 --> 43:18.626 align:middle
You know, it's, it's not
something we can control

43:18.626 --> 43:21.696 align:middle
or operate on directly, but we
do-we do watch those things.

43:22.336 --> 43:23.116 align:middle
And, you know, in a sense,

43:23.116 --> 43:24.646 align:middle
it's good that companies
have been able

43:24.646 --> 43:26.316 align:middle
to finance themselves
during this period,

43:26.316 --> 43:28.156 align:middle
because they-they've been
able to stay in business.

43:28.156 --> 43:31.156 align:middle
They've been able to keep
their employees working.

43:31.786 --> 43:33.176 align:middle
And, you know, that's
a good thing.

43:33.176 --> 43:33.946 align:middle
And that's, that's part

43:33.946 --> 43:35.726 align:middle
of highly accommodative
financial conditions.

43:35.726 --> 43:36.296 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

43:38.846 --> 43:40.146 align:middle
Thanks. Chris Rugaber.

43:43.746 --> 43:44.326 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

43:44.326 --> 43:49.916 align:middle
Thanks. Can you-since you
suggested that the concern

43:49.916 --> 43:52.836 align:middle
from the pandemic is perhaps
a shorter-term concern

43:52.836 --> 43:55.126 align:middle
and that's why you removed
some of the language

43:55.126 --> 43:57.986 align:middle
from the statement, can
you talk about what kind

43:57.986 --> 44:00.196 align:middle
of impact you're seeing so far?

44:00.196 --> 44:04.656 align:middle
Do you feel that structural
impacts have been-how would you

44:04.656 --> 44:05.496 align:middle
characterize those?

44:05.496 --> 44:06.996 align:middle
I know that's been a major
concern-you've talked

44:06.996 --> 44:08.666 align:middle
about at this press
conference and others.

44:09.196 --> 44:12.036 align:middle
Do we see longer-term
damage to the job market?

44:12.036 --> 44:15.916 align:middle
Have-or has there been some
success in preventing that?

44:15.916 --> 44:18.776 align:middle
And, compared to
previous recessions,

44:18.856 --> 44:21.686 align:middle
how would you characterize the
damage to the job market here,

44:21.686 --> 44:25.126 align:middle
in terms of things like, you
know, permanent job loss,

44:25.126 --> 44:28.016 align:middle
workers that need to shift
industries, that kind of thing?

44:28.016 --> 44:29.406 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

44:29.566 --> 44:32.236 align:middle
You know, the, the, the big
thing is that the jury's out.

44:32.516 --> 44:35.696 align:middle
And this has been a concern
since the very beginning,

44:35.696 --> 44:40.716 align:middle
is the concern that people-if,
if they become disconnected

44:40.716 --> 44:43.946 align:middle
from the industry or the job
where-that they used to work in,

44:44.496 --> 44:48.936 align:middle
it, it can be years or
never when they get back

44:48.936 --> 44:50.876 align:middle
into the labor force,
particularly for people

44:50.876 --> 44:54.326 align:middle
who are well along in
their careers-the same kind

44:54.326 --> 44:55.986 align:middle
of scarring for small
businesses,

44:55.986 --> 44:57.566 align:middle
which don't have the
kind of resources

44:57.566 --> 44:58.656 align:middle
that you need to
get through this.

44:59.376 --> 45:01.586 align:middle
So we've, we've had
a big concern about,

45:01.666 --> 45:02.836 align:middle
about both of those.

45:03.336 --> 45:06.516 align:middle
We haven't seen as much
of it as we-as we feared.

45:06.516 --> 45:07.576 align:middle
And that's a good thing.

45:07.646 --> 45:11.006 align:middle
But as I said that, you
know, the jury is out here-9

45:11.006 --> 45:16.096 align:middle
or 10 million people still out
of work because of the pandemic.

45:16.926 --> 45:19.236 align:middle
They're-a big chunk of
them are people who worked

45:19.356 --> 45:22.066 align:middle
in public-facing jobs
in the service sector.

45:23.106 --> 45:26.176 align:middle
And, you know, they've
gotten a lot of support

45:26.656 --> 45:30.706 align:middle
from fiscal policy and
some from monetary policy.

45:30.756 --> 45:33.446 align:middle
But the question is
getting them back to work.

45:33.766 --> 45:38.496 align:middle
And, you know, it's, it's harder
if you're-it takes longer,

45:38.496 --> 45:41.306 align:middle
empirically, to find that
next job if you're looking

45:41.306 --> 45:42.356 align:middle
at a brand-new industry.

45:42.916 --> 45:47.566 align:middle
It's not easy to change
careers completely midcareer.

45:49.156 --> 45:53.916 align:middle
So that just, again, stresses
the urgency that we feel

45:53.916 --> 45:55.946 align:middle
and others feel at,
at, you know,

45:55.946 --> 45:58.996 align:middle
fully defeating the
pandemic, finishing the job

45:59.666 --> 46:02.776 align:middle
and getting back to a
place where it's safe

46:03.006 --> 46:06.496 align:middle
to have these-you know,
to, to, to stay in hotels,

46:06.496 --> 46:09.556 align:middle
to fly on airplanes, to
go to sporting events

46:09.556 --> 46:11.246 align:middle
and movie theaters, and
all of those things.

46:11.936 --> 46:15.106 align:middle
So those people are
still at risk.

46:15.236 --> 46:18.426 align:middle
And we're, we're very, very
focused on that group of people

46:19.026 --> 46:22.666 align:middle
with our policy and,
and, and, you know,

46:23.616 --> 46:25.696 align:middle
the way we look at the economy.

46:26.176 --> 46:29.536 align:middle
So many people have had that
bridge across the pandemic.

46:29.536 --> 46:31.046 align:middle
You know, we talked about
that at the beginning.

46:31.306 --> 46:34.116 align:middle
For many people, it's clear
that they've made it across,

46:34.116 --> 46:36.126 align:middle
and their job is okay,
and their house is okay.

46:36.706 --> 46:38.826 align:middle
And, you know, it's been
terribly inconvenient

46:38.826 --> 46:41.806 align:middle
and painful, and schools are
closed and things like that.

46:41.806 --> 46:43.166 align:middle
But there, there's
a bunch of people

46:43.166 --> 46:44.796 align:middle
yet who haven't found
that bridge yet.

46:44.796 --> 46:47.326 align:middle
And we're, we're
very focused on that.

46:47.326 --> 46:48.406 align:middle
Of course, the other thing is,

46:49.036 --> 46:50.456 align:middle
we're going to a
different economy.

46:51.146 --> 46:54.206 align:middle
And we're going to be learning
more about that as we go.

46:54.206 --> 46:59.366 align:middle
But, clearly, we're, we're
learning that things can be done

46:59.516 --> 47:01.076 align:middle
from remote locations.

47:01.076 --> 47:04.146 align:middle
We're learning that technology
can replace people even more

47:04.146 --> 47:04.796 align:middle
than we thought.

47:05.566 --> 47:06.756 align:middle
And some of that is happening.

47:06.756 --> 47:12.236 align:middle
So I think as we, you know,
as we get into this, you know,

47:12.296 --> 47:15.966 align:middle
the phase after-even after
the economy fully reopens,

47:15.966 --> 47:17.826 align:middle
I think we're still going
to need to keep people

47:17.826 --> 47:20.736 align:middle
in mind whose lives
have been disrupted,

47:20.736 --> 47:23.086 align:middle
because they've lost
that-the work that they did.

47:23.676 --> 47:26.516 align:middle
And I think it would
be wise as a country,

47:26.516 --> 47:28.786 align:middle
for the longer-run productive
capacity of the country,

47:29.376 --> 47:30.986 align:middle
if we were to look
out for those people

47:30.986 --> 47:33.436 align:middle
and help them find their way
back into the labor force even

47:33.436 --> 47:35.286 align:middle
if it means continuing support

47:35.286 --> 47:37.856 align:middle
for an additional
period of time.

47:38.286 --> 47:39.046 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

47:39.046 --> 47:43.296 align:middle
Great. If I could ask this quick
follow-yesterday Susan Rice,

47:43.476 --> 47:45.246 align:middle
domestic policy advisor
at the White House,

47:45.386 --> 47:46.956 align:middle
talked about a Citigroup study

47:46.956 --> 47:49.926 align:middle
that said closing racial
economic gaps could add

47:49.926 --> 47:54.166 align:middle
up to $5 trillion to the U.S.
economy over the next five years

47:54.166 --> 47:55.536 align:middle
and 6 million new jobs.

47:55.706 --> 47:58.876 align:middle
Do you agree that racial
disparities are currently a drag

47:58.876 --> 47:59.796 align:middle
on the economy?

47:59.796 --> 48:02.156 align:middle
And, if so, what can
the Fed do proactively

48:02.726 --> 48:05.126 align:middle
to narrow those racial
gaps in income and wealth,

48:05.126 --> 48:07.496 align:middle
including potentially
through regulatory policy?

48:07.676 --> 48:07.936 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

48:08.296 --> 48:10.456 align:middle
I strongly agree with
that, as a matter of fact.

48:10.456 --> 48:15.806 align:middle
I think if you look at
either-look at employment gaps

48:15.806 --> 48:19.256 align:middle
or unemployment gaps or
wage gaps, wealth gaps,

48:20.046 --> 48:23.296 align:middle
homeowning gaps, all of
those persistent gaps

48:23.296 --> 48:28.296 align:middle
that exist even controlling for
many other factors, you will see

48:28.296 --> 48:29.616 align:middle
that they're, they're
persistent,

48:29.616 --> 48:31.736 align:middle
they're-and they're very
difficult to explain.

48:33.296 --> 48:39.986 align:middle
And so it's-the reason we
talk about inequality and,

48:39.986 --> 48:43.296 align:middle
and racial inequality in
particular, is that it goes

48:43.296 --> 48:47.476 align:middle
to our job, which is to achieve
maximum employment, which,

48:47.596 --> 48:51.056 align:middle
which links up with, with-we
want-we want the potential

48:51.056 --> 48:53.356 align:middle
output of the United
States to be as high

48:53.356 --> 48:54.506 align:middle
as it can possibly be.

48:54.506 --> 48:57.506 align:middle
We want-we want an economy
where everybody can take part

48:57.846 --> 49:00.096 align:middle
and everybody, everybody
can put their labor in,

49:00.096 --> 49:03.086 align:middle
and they can share in the
prosperity of our great economy.

49:03.086 --> 49:03.936 align:middle
That's what we want.

49:04.176 --> 49:07.886 align:middle
And that's how the U.S.
economy can be bigger, stronger,

49:08.246 --> 49:12.796 align:middle
growing faster, is if
we can achieve a more-a

49:12.796 --> 49:13.986 align:middle
broader prosperity.

49:13.986 --> 49:17.826 align:middle
So, now, in terms of our
tools, you know, of course,

49:17.826 --> 49:19.856 align:middle
that project that I
just mentioned is one

49:19.856 --> 49:22.026 align:middle
for the broader society,
for the private sector,

49:22.026 --> 49:24.356 align:middle
for fiscal policy, but
we have a role to play.

49:24.356 --> 49:27.326 align:middle
And, and that is, how we
think about the labor market.

49:27.986 --> 49:31.786 align:middle
Principally, when we say that
the maximum employment is a,

49:32.666 --> 49:35.046 align:middle
a broad and inclusive goal,

49:35.586 --> 49:37.906 align:middle
what we're-what we're saying
there is, we're not just going

49:37.906 --> 49:40.206 align:middle
to look at the headline-and
we never did, really,

49:40.206 --> 49:42.206 align:middle
but-we're not going to just
look at the headline numbers.

49:42.206 --> 49:44.136 align:middle
We're going to look at
different demographic groups,

49:44.506 --> 49:46.416 align:middle
including women,
minorities, and others.

49:46.416 --> 49:47.636 align:middle
And we're not going to say

49:47.966 --> 49:49.806 align:middle
that we've reached
maximum employment,

49:49.806 --> 49:51.116 align:middle
which is our statutory goal,

49:51.526 --> 49:53.656 align:middle
until we've reached
maximum employment.

49:53.656 --> 49:56.756 align:middle
And you haven't if, if
there's lots of pockets of,

49:56.756 --> 49:58.516 align:middle
of people not participating in

49:58.516 --> 50:00.776 align:middle
or not employed in
the labor market.

50:00.776 --> 50:02.136 align:middle
So those are the
things that we can do.

50:02.136 --> 50:05.796 align:middle
We also do a tremendous amount
of research on these issues.

50:05.796 --> 50:09.416 align:middle
You know, we do-we, we have
such a focus on these issues now

50:09.416 --> 50:12.916 align:middle
that we actually-we, we have a
new, a webpage, relatively new,

50:12.916 --> 50:15.006 align:middle
where you can access all of it.

50:15.006 --> 50:18.176 align:middle
But it's a lot of activities
in our Division of Consumer

50:18.176 --> 50:19.146 align:middle
and Community Affairs.

50:19.546 --> 50:24.076 align:middle
It's lots of research, and,
and it's also, you know,

50:24.076 --> 50:26.406 align:middle
our enforcement of
the fair lending laws

50:26.406 --> 50:27.086 align:middle
and things like that.

50:27.086 --> 50:29.746 align:middle
So I think everybody's
got a role to play here.

50:29.746 --> 50:32.506 align:middle
It's a-it's a national
goal and a national job,

50:32.506 --> 50:34.506 align:middle
and we're just going to
do our part of the job.

50:35.136 --> 50:37.086 align:middle
And it's something we're
strongly committed to.

50:37.086 --> 50:37.726 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

50:39.266 --> 50:39.966 align:middle
Thank you.

50:39.966 --> 50:42.796 align:middle
For the last question, we'll
go to Scott Horsley at NPR.

50:42.916 --> 50:45.406 align:middle
SCOTT HORSLEY.

50:45.406 --> 50:46.236 align:middle
Thanks, Mr. Chairman.

50:46.326 --> 50:48.736 align:middle
You said in your
opening remarks that the,

50:49.816 --> 50:53.476 align:middle
the economy in many ways had
proven more resilient than,

50:53.526 --> 50:57.216 align:middle
than people expected, and that
that reflected the adaptability

50:57.216 --> 50:59.066 align:middle
of both households
and businesses.

50:59.716 --> 51:03.986 align:middle
Are, are there adaptations
that caught you by surprise,

51:03.986 --> 51:09.646 align:middle
maybe related to remote work
or new ways to do on-site work,

51:09.646 --> 51:11.246 align:middle
in-person, face-to-face
work that,

51:11.346 --> 51:15.876 align:middle
that the economy's proven
tougher than, than you expected

51:15.876 --> 51:16.786 align:middle
at the outset of this pandemic?

51:17.236 --> 51:17.976 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

51:17.976 --> 51:18.526 align:middle
Well, yes.

51:18.626 --> 51:20.846 align:middle
So I think, if you go back
to the-to the beginning,

51:21.586 --> 51:22.876 align:middle
there was a real concern.

51:22.876 --> 51:24.996 align:middle
I mean, just, just take the
financial markets, for example.

51:24.996 --> 51:28.296 align:middle
Suddenly, all of those big
buildings all over the,

51:28.296 --> 51:32.956 align:middle
the-New York City and all around
the world where people work

51:32.956 --> 51:35.026 align:middle
in the financial markets with
terminals and everything,

51:35.596 --> 51:36.816 align:middle
everybody had to go
home, and they had

51:36.816 --> 51:38.066 align:middle
to take their terminals
with them.

51:38.266 --> 51:39.676 align:middle
And I think there
was a real concern

51:39.676 --> 51:42.566 align:middle
that there would be a tremendous
loss of functionality just

51:42.566 --> 51:44.906 align:middle
at the time when the
financial markets were

51:44.906 --> 51:47.846 align:middle
under historically
difficult conditions.

51:48.186 --> 51:50.166 align:middle
And yet, it worked out okay.

51:50.166 --> 51:52.006 align:middle
So I think people-many people

51:52.006 --> 51:53.836 align:middle
in the financial
sector are still working

51:54.406 --> 51:56.456 align:middle
with their terminals at
home, including people

51:56.456 --> 51:59.006 align:middle
on trading floors or on
virtual trading floors.

51:59.006 --> 52:00.306 align:middle
Now, some of that's
reversing now.

52:00.306 --> 52:04.046 align:middle
But we have definitely learned
that we can do more work

52:04.046 --> 52:07.506 align:middle
from home in many, many
different lines of work.

52:07.506 --> 52:10.476 align:middle
Of course, that's not possible
if your job involves being

52:10.476 --> 52:12.846 align:middle
at a place, doing
personal services.

52:12.846 --> 52:14.616 align:middle
For many people,
it's not possible.

52:14.616 --> 52:18.026 align:middle
And that's very much something
that skews to higher-income,

52:18.026 --> 52:20.496 align:middle
higher-educated people
being able to work from home

52:20.496 --> 52:23.776 align:middle
and others not so much.

52:23.836 --> 52:26.276 align:middle
So I think we've learned that.

52:26.276 --> 52:29.286 align:middle
The other thing, though,
even conditional on that,

52:29.756 --> 52:32.096 align:middle
when we saw the wave in
the South and the West,

52:32.096 --> 52:36.136 align:middle
the wave of cases this
summer, I think, intuitively,

52:36.386 --> 52:38.296 align:middle
having seen what happened
in March and April,

52:38.736 --> 52:41.456 align:middle
we expected there to
be a significant hit

52:41.456 --> 52:42.706 align:middle
to economic activity.

52:43.456 --> 52:46.156 align:middle
And people kind of just
got on with their lives

52:46.156 --> 52:47.796 align:middle
and went and, and dealt with it.

52:47.796 --> 52:50.476 align:middle
And, and it didn't actually-it
had a much smaller effect

52:50.476 --> 52:53.326 align:middle
on economic activity
than we expected.

52:54.096 --> 52:57.476 align:middle
Then comes the fall wave, which
is just so much larger-a very,

52:57.476 --> 53:00.516 align:middle
very large wave as, as
was very much forecast.

53:00.566 --> 53:03.586 align:middle
People going indoors, the
cold weather, all of that.

53:03.586 --> 53:06.136 align:middle
And even there, if you
look at-I would say,

53:06.136 --> 53:08.206 align:middle
look at the December
jobs report.

53:08.206 --> 53:12.026 align:middle
So, big job losses in,
you know, in that part

53:12.026 --> 53:12.876 align:middle
of the service sector.

53:12.876 --> 53:16.096 align:middle
So I mentioned 400,000 jobs
in bars and restaurants.

53:16.096 --> 53:19.646 align:middle
There's another 100,000 in
similar kinds of activities.

53:19.676 --> 53:21.836 align:middle
But if you go-if
you look elsewhere,

53:23.056 --> 53:24.116 align:middle
it's not having an effect.

53:24.326 --> 53:26.186 align:middle
You know, even purchasing
manager index

53:26.186 --> 53:29.396 align:middle
and sentiment indexes on,
on areas of the economy

53:29.396 --> 53:33.346 align:middle
that are not directly-really
directly-exposed to the pandemic

53:33.346 --> 53:36.146 align:middle
in their economic
activity, they're doing okay.

53:36.416 --> 53:38.446 align:middle
They are. Housing
is a great example.

53:38.886 --> 53:41.786 align:middle
You know, the way-the way-the
way the housing industry worked,

53:41.786 --> 53:44.306 align:middle
that when you buy a house, there
was a lot of in-person contact.

53:44.676 --> 53:48.116 align:middle
They managed to pretty
much immediately go

53:48.116 --> 53:51.576 align:middle
to a more virtual-you know,
with all the technology.

53:52.056 --> 53:54.436 align:middle
They were able to
completely avoid that.

53:54.436 --> 53:56.986 align:middle
And the housing market has been
really strong, notwithstanding

53:57.176 --> 54:01.426 align:middle
that it's, it's now quite
virtual rather than in person.

54:01.426 --> 54:04.736 align:middle
So there's been a
lot of adapting,

54:04.736 --> 54:10.396 align:middle
and-but you can't adapt, you
know, hotels, sporting venues,

54:10.666 --> 54:13.046 align:middle
movie theaters, restaurants,
bars.

54:13.046 --> 54:16.926 align:middle
You just-you know, those are
just going to-and that's-again,

54:17.086 --> 54:19.096 align:middle
that's millions and
millions of people.

54:19.836 --> 54:23.476 align:middle
And so you're just going to have
to defeat the pandemic, which,

54:23.476 --> 54:26.746 align:middle
as I mentioned, you know, we,
we have a plan to do that,

54:26.806 --> 54:28.186 align:middle
but we haven't done it yet.

54:28.186 --> 54:30.706 align:middle
And we need to-we need
to finish the job.

54:30.946 --> 54:32.656 align:middle
And we-it's, it's
within our power to do

54:32.656 --> 54:34.046 align:middle
that as a country this year.

54:34.106 --> 54:37.976 align:middle
And I would just urge that-and
I know people are working hard

54:37.976 --> 54:38.266 align:middle
on that.

54:38.266 --> 54:40.776 align:middle
But that, that is really the
main thing about the economy,

54:41.426 --> 54:43.966 align:middle
is, is getting the
pandemic under control,

54:43.966 --> 54:45.256 align:middle
getting everyone vaccinated,

54:45.356 --> 54:46.996 align:middle
getting people wearing
masks, and all that.

54:46.996 --> 54:49.796 align:middle
That's the single most
important economic growth policy

54:50.556 --> 54:52.846 align:middle
that we can have.

54:53.816 --> 54:56.136 align:middle
Thank you.

